WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.138
After emission, telemetry becomes
security-sensitive stored data.

2
00:00:04.218 --> 00:00:08.411
Protect it in transit and at rest,
separate production from development access,

3
00:00:08.491 --> 00:00:11.081
and grant operators, developers, support
staff,

4
00:00:11.161 --> 00:00:13.936
and vendors only the visibility their
roles require.

5
00:00:14.016 --> 00:00:17.963
Retention should balance incident and
audit value against exposure, cost,

6
00:00:18.043 --> 00:00:19.954
privacy, and deletion obligations.

7
00:00:20.034 --> 00:00:24.413
Centralized append-oriented collection can
improve availability and make casual

8
00:00:24.493 --> 00:00:26.959
modification harder than mutable local
files,

9
00:00:27.039 --> 00:00:31.603
but ordinary centralized logging is not
automatically tamper-proof or sufficient for

10
00:00:31.683 --> 00:00:33.409
high-assurance audit evidence.

11
00:00:33.489 --> 00:00:36.511
Design failure behavior according to event
criticality.

12
00:00:36.591 --> 00:00:39.612
A dropped debug event should not normally
crash a request,

13
00:00:39.692 --> 00:00:43.639
while a regulated or security-critical
audit workflow may require durable

14
00:00:43.719 --> 00:00:46.617
acknowledgement or a business-specific
failure path.

15
00:00:46.697 --> 00:00:48.424
Make that distinction explicit.

16
00:00:48.504 --> 00:00:52.635
Asynchronous exporters need bounded
queues, finite retry budgets, timeouts,

17
00:00:52.715 --> 00:00:54.997
drop counters, and backpressure policies.

18
00:00:55.077 --> 00:00:58.777
Unbounded buffering or infinite retries
can turn a collector outage or

19
00:00:58.857 --> 00:01:03.173
attacker-generated event flood into memory
exhaustion and application failure.

20
00:01:03.253 --> 00:01:07.755
Metrics should reveal drops and saturation
without using unbounded user-controlled

21
00:01:07.835 --> 00:01:08.267
labels.

22
00:01:08.347 --> 00:01:12.416
Test the data leaving the logger, not only
whether a logging method was called.

23
00:01:12.496 --> 00:01:16.196
Capture the serialized output and verify
that authorization headers,

24
00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:18.805
cookies, secrets, unknown request
properties,

25
00:01:18.885 --> 00:01:21.290
and disallowed exception details are
absent.

26
00:01:21.370 --> 00:01:24.514
Submit values containing newlines and
control characters,

27
00:01:24.594 --> 00:01:29.034
then confirm they remain one structured
field through serialization and ingestion.

28
00:01:29.114 --> 00:01:32.321
Verify correlation identifiers are
normalized and bounded,

29
00:01:32.401 --> 00:01:34.251
expected event fields are present,

30
00:01:34.331 --> 00:01:38.524
and a future secret-looking request
property does not appear simply because the

31
00:01:38.604 --> 00:01:39.837
request model changed.

32
00:01:39.917 --> 00:01:41.644
Test operational semantics too.

33
00:01:41.724 --> 00:01:45.547
Confirm a high-value audit event is
emitted exactly once where required,

34
00:01:45.627 --> 00:01:48.156
including retry and duplicate-delivery
paths.

35
00:01:48.236 --> 00:01:52.367
Simulate transport loss, full buffers,
exporter exceptions, and rate spikes;

36
00:01:52.447 --> 00:01:56.825
verify the application follows the
documented policy and remains within memory and

37
00:01:56.905 --> 00:01:57.769
latency bounds.

38
00:01:57.849 --> 00:02:02.289
Validate access controls and retention
configuration outside the code repository.

39
00:02:02.369 --> 00:02:05.513
Secure telemetry is an end-to-end property
of event design,

40
00:02:05.593 --> 00:02:09.540
serialization, transport, storage,
presentation, access, and deletion.
